-# Smart TV Remote 0.6.5
+# Smart TV Remote 0.6.6
 
 Local Wi-Fi remote for:
 
@@ -7,16 +7,19 @@
 
 The Android app and Windows tester use the same local controller and responsive interface. No companion app is installed on the television.
 
-## Fixes in 0.6.5
+## Fixes in 0.6.6
 
-- Samsung authorization tokens are now read as either strings or numbers and are also found in nested connection responses. Some Tizen models return a numeric token, which older builds silently discarded and therefore requested approval again after a restart.
-- Samsung TVs with token authentication stay on the secure port 8002 instead of falling back to port 8001, where persistent authorization is not available on newer models.
-- Added WebSocket ping keepalive, correct close-frame handling and session-safe cleanup so a dead socket is never shown as connected.
-- Added automatic reconnection after switching to HDMI, opening an application, temporary Wi-Fi interruption or waking the TV. The saved token is retained while the TV is off.
-- Reconnection stops after a genuine authorization rejection, preventing an endless loop of approval popups. A manual Connect then requests a new token once.
-- Samsung YouTube, Netflix and Internet launch now try the installed app ID, modern and legacy Tizen IDs, the remote channel, the separate app-control WebSocket, REST and remote-key fallbacks. DIAL is only the final fallback and is no longer treated as success without a visible app-state confirmation.
-- Added the modern YouTube ID `9Ur5IzDKqV.TizenYouTube` and the native Samsung Browser launch form `org.tizen.browser` with a URL meta tag.
-- Samsung REST information and app-state checks now work on both HTTP 8001 and HTTPS 8002.
+- Restored Samsung text entry. The app now tracks Tizen `ms.remote.imeStart` and `ms.remote.imeEnd` sessions instead of blindly closing every text session immediately.
+- Samsung text entry first tries the secure IME REST endpoint when a permanent token is available, then falls back to the WebSocket `SendInputString` protocol.
+- UTF-8 text is encoded correctly as Base64, and the text-session announcement is sent only when needed.
+- Netflix, YouTube and Internet no longer open several temporary app-control WebSockets during the same transition. This prevented connection saturation and reduced cases where Tizen closed the main remote channel after launching Netflix.
+- The installed application ID reported by the TV is preferred before generic fallback IDs, including Samsung Internet.
+- Application launch now stops after the first accepted command when the firmware cannot report active-app state, instead of repeatedly sending conflicting launch methods.
+- The app watches the full 15-second application/HDMI transition window and automatically restores the Samsung remote channel if Netflix or another app closes it several seconds later.
+- A successful launch command is never repeated merely because the TV closed the channel during the application transition.
+- Samsung authorization is preserved across DHCP IP changes and temporary REST failures. A token is removed only when the TV positively identifies as a different device or rejects it repeatedly.
+- A temporarily rejected token is retried after the app transition before the user is asked to approve the phone again.
+- Configuration, including the Samsung token, is written through a temporary file and backup to reduce the chance of losing authorization during an interrupted write.
 - Netflix and PlayStation icons remain constrained to their buttons on mobile screens.
 
 ## Samsung first connection
